problems.problem:
The plane is divided into unit squares like a sheet of squared paper; the side length of each square is $1$. A pentagon is drawn whose vertices all lie at vertices of the grid. Prove that:
(a) its area is at least $\frac{3}{2}$;
(b) if the pentagon is convex, then its area is at least $\frac{5}{2}$.

Prove that for every positive integer $n$:
(a) $1^3+2^3+\dots+n^3$ is divisible by $1+2+\dots+n$;
(b) $1^5+2^5+\dots+n^5$ is divisible by $1+2+\dots+n$.

Circles $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ with centres $O_1$ and $O_2$ respectively intersect at points $A$ and $B$. The rays $O_1B$ and $O_2B$ meet $\omega_2$ and $\omega_1$ again at points $E$ and $F$ respectively. The line through $B$ parallel to $EF$ meets $\omega_1$ and $\omega_2$ again at points $M$ and $N$ respectively. Prove that:
(a) $A$, $O_1$, $O_2$, $E$, $F$ lie on one circle;
(b) $MN=AE+AF$.

The $10$ volumes of the collected works of Professor Cipariņš stand on a shelf in one row. In one move it is allowed to take any number of adjacent volumes (possibly a single volume) and, keeping them in the same order and without turning them around, put them anywhere on the shelf (at one end, at the other end, or between two volumes remaining on the shelf). Professor Cipariņš wants to rearrange the volumes in reverse order. Find a positive integer $x$ as small as possible and a positive integer $y$ as large as possible with the following properties:
(a) Cipariņš can achieve his goal in $x$ moves;
(b) Cipariņš cannot achieve his goal in $y$ moves.

There are $1001$ one-lats coins on a table. Each of them may lie either with the coat of arms or with the salmon facing up. $1000$ moves are made. In the $n$-th move ($n=1, 2, 3, \dots, 1000$) one chooses $n$ coins and turns them over. Prove that:
(a) one can achieve that after all the moves have been made, all coins lie with the same side up;
(b) whether the coins will lie with the coat of arms or with the salmon facing up is uniquely determined by the initial position of the coins.

A point $C$ is an interior point of a segment $AB$. Equilateral triangles $ACB_1$ and $CBA_1$ lie on one side of the line $AB$, and an equilateral triangle $ABC_1$ lies on the other side. The lines $AA_1$ and $BB_1$ meet at a point $S$. Prove that:
(a) $\angle B_1SA=60^\circ$;
(b) the points $S$, $C$ and $C_1$ lie on one line.

About a sequence of numbers $x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots$ it is known that
$x_1=1$;
$x_{2n}=1+x_n$ for all positive integers $n$;
$x_{2n+1}=\frac{1}{x_{2n}}$ for all positive integers $n$.
(a) Prove that all terms of the sequence are distinct.
(b) Which numbers are terms of this sequence?

Each vertex of a regular $1000$-gon is coloured white, red or green. In one move it is allowed to choose two adjacent vertices that are coloured in different colours and recolour both of them in the third colour. Prove that:
one can achieve that all vertices are coloured in one and the same colour;
this "final colour" is uniquely determined by the initial colouring.

On a certain planet $2009$ languages are used. Can a system of dictionaries be created so that the following $3$ properties hold simultaneously:
(a) each dictionary allows one to translate from one language into some other, but not in the opposite direction;
(b) if there is a dictionary that allows one to translate from some language $A$ into some other language $B$, then there is no dictionary that allows one to translate from $B$ into $A$;
(c) from every language into every other one can translate using either one or two dictionaries? (Repeated translations are allowed, for example, from $A$ into $B$ and further from $B$ into $C$.)

A string of letters containing only the letters $a$, $b$ and $c$ is written on a blackboard. The following moves may be performed with this string:
- change the order of the written letters arbitrarily;
- if the fragment $ab$ is written at the end of the string, it may be erased;
- replace a fragment $ba$ with the fragment $aabbcc$;
- replace a fragment $bbc$ with $a$;
- delete any three equal letters written consecutively.
Is it possible, by repeating such moves several times, to obtain the string $aba$ if initially the string written is
1) $abba$;
2) $aabbcabaab$?

In the Votivapu language all words consist only of the two letters $a$ and $b$. Every word can be obtained from the word "$a$" by repeatedly applying the following three rules:
1) appending the letter $b$ at the end of the word;
2) appending the word itself at its end;
3) replacing three consecutive letters $a$ in the word by one letter $b$.
Are the following words in the Votivapu language: (a) $abbababab$; (b) $baabaabaa$?

Two polynomials $P(x)=x^2+2$ and $Q(x)=x+1$ are written on a blackboard. In one move Ilmārs chooses two polynomials $a$ and $b$ already written on the blackboard ($a$ and $b$ may also be one and the same polynomial) and writes on the blackboard one of the polynomials $a+b$, $a-b$ or $a\cdot b$ (the result may also be a polynomial of degree zero, which is a number). Can he, by repeating such operations, at some moment succeed in writing on the blackboard the polynomial
(a) $x^3+2$;
(b) $x^4+2$?
